data.reasoningText:
Good. Now I have a clear picture of the project. It's a .NET 11 Blazor Web App with Interactive Server render mode. Bootstrap is available.

Let me plan the component decomposition and then implement everything.

## Component Plan: Settings Dashboard

### Component Tree
```
Settings                    (page — owns active section, orchestrates layout)
├── SettingsSidebar         (vertical nav tabs)
├── ProfileSection          (profile editing)
├── NotificationsSection    (notification preferences)
├── SecuritySection         (security settings)
│   └── RevokeSessionDialog (confirmation modal)
└── AppearanceSection       (appearance settings with live preview)
```

I'm reconsidering the data flow — each section should independently load and save its own data through the service rather than having the page manage everything. The page just needs to track which section is active, and each component injects the service directly to handle its own state. This keeps things cleaner and more modular.

Now I'll start building out the models.
```
I need to create the service interface and models first since they're not in the project yet, even though the user mentioned they're already registered in DI. I'll create the models and interface files, implement a mock service, then build out all the settings components and register everything in Program.cs. Rather than invoking skills for each component, I'll implement them directly for efficiency.
```

csharp
// Services/ISettingsService.cs
public interface ISettingsService
{
    Task<ProfileSettings> GetProfileAsync();
    Task SaveProfileAsync(ProfileSettings settings);
    Task<NotificationSettings> GetNotificationsAsync();
    Task SaveNotificationsAsync(NotificationSettings settings);
    Task<SecuritySettings> GetSecurityAsync();
    Task SaveSecurityAsync(SecuritySettings settings);
    Task<AppearanceSettings> GetAppearanceAsync();
    Task SaveAppearanceAsync(AppearanceSettings settings);
}

public record ProfileSettings(string DisplayName, string Email, string Bio, string AvatarUrl);
public record NotificationSettings(bool EmailNotifications, bool PushNotifications, bool WeeklyDigest);
public record SecuritySettings(string LastPasswordChange, bool TwoFactorEnabled);
public record AppearanceSettings(string Theme, string FontSize);
```

Now I'm implementing the mock service that returns sample data and simulates saves, then creating the Settings page with a sidebar layout and four content sections for profile, notifications, security, and appearance. Each section will have its own component that loads data from the service, displays form fields, and handles save/discard actions with success messages.

